    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No trial has ever measured acne scars after an oral collagen supplement, so collagen cannot honestly be called a scar treatment.</strong> Atrophic scars are lost dermal structure and respond to procedures; post-acne dark marks are melanin and respond to topicals and peels. Oral collagen's measured effects are elasticity and hydration.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Zero direct evidence:</strong> search the literature for oral collagen and acne scarring and no randomised trial returns with scar depth, scar count or mark severity as an endpoint.</li>
        <li><strong>Two different problems:</strong> ice pick, boxcar and rolling scars are lost collagen; brown or grey post-acne marks are pigment. They need different treatments.</li>
        <li><strong>What the trials support:</strong> for atrophic scars, a 2026 network meta-analysis of 56 randomised trials in 1,488 patients found combinations such as fractional CO<sub>2</sub> laser with subcision outperformed single treatments.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> even the Cochrane review of 24 trials and 789 people could not name a first-line treatment, and found no evidence that short-term gains last.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

