      <h2 id="does-collagen-help">Does collagen help acne scars?</h2>
      <p>On the evidence available today, no. Not because a trial tested it and failed, but because no trial has tested it at all. Search the published literature for oral collagen supplementation and acne scarring and nothing comes back with scarring as a measured outcome.</p>
      <p>That is an unusual thing for a collagen company to put in writing, so it is worth being precise about what the gap means. It does not mean collagen is useless; our own <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">review of collagen for skin</a> covers the endpoints where it has been measured. It means that anyone telling you a supplement reduces scar depth over eight to twelve weeks is describing something nobody has recorded.</p>
      <p>There is a related body of work on wound healing that gets borrowed to fill the gap, and it should not be. A 2020 pilot randomised trial in <em>Burns</em> gave roughly 31 burn patients a hydrolysed collagen supplement or an isocaloric placebo for four weeks and reported a hazard ratio of 3.7 for wound healing (95% CI 1.43 to 9.52). Helping a fresh wound close is a different biological event from remodelling a scar that set two years ago, and commentators on the topic are consistent that no study has shown oral or topical collagen improves the appearance of established scars.</p>

