      <h2 id="scar-or-mark">Is it a scar or a dark mark?</h2>
      <p>This is the question that decides everything else, and almost no article on the subject asks it. Most people who say "acne scars" are looking at one of two completely different things in the mirror, and a treatment that helps one does nothing for the other.</p>
      <p>A true acne scar is a change in the shape of the skin. Run a finger over it and there is a dip. Fabbrocini and colleagues, writing in <em>Dermatology Research and Practice</em> in 2010, classify these atrophic scars into three shapes: ice pick, which they put at 60 to 70% of atrophic scars, boxcar at 20 to 30%, and rolling at 15 to 25%. Those are lost dermal collagen and tethering, and they do not fade.</p>
      <p>A post-acne mark is a change in the colour of the skin, with no change in texture. In Indian skin this is usually post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation: melanin left behind after the inflammation settles. It can take months, but it generally does fade.</p>

