      <h2 id="what-works-scars">What actually works for atrophic acne scars?</h2>
      <p>Procedures, and usually more than one of them. The strongest recent evidence is a 2026 systematic review and network meta-analysis of 56 randomised trials covering 1,488 patients with moderate-to-severe atrophic acne scars. Its headline finding was that combination therapies generally outperformed monotherapies, with fractional CO<sub>2</sub> laser paired with subcision or platelet-rich plasma showing the most consistent benefit. The authors are explicit that the right choice depends on scar type, Fitzpatrick skin phototype, downtime and adverse-effect risk — which is why this is a consultation, not a shopping decision.</p>
      <p>Matching the tool to the shape matters more than picking the newest device:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Rolling scars:</strong> subcision releases the fibrous tethers pulling the surface down, and microneedling is the most consistently reported option for rolling and shallow boxcar types.</li>
        <li><strong>Boxcar scars:</strong> fractional resurfacing for shallower ones; punch techniques for deep, sharply walled scars.</li>
        <li><strong>Ice pick scars:</strong> the hardest group. Focal chemical application (TCA into the pit) or punch excision, because resurfacing the whole face cannot reach the base of a narrow deep pit.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed scarring, which is most people:</strong> sequential sessions combining modalities, plus topical retinoids at home.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Two honest caveats. A 2025 meta-analysis comparing fractional CO<sub>2</sub> laser with needling-based modalities found no statistically significant difference between them in mean scar reduction, so the expensive option is not automatically the better one. And the 2016 Cochrane review of 24 randomised trials in 789 adults concluded that the evidence did not establish first-line use of any single treatment, and found no evidence that short-term benefits last long term. Expect improvement, not erasure.</p>

      <h2 id="what-works-marks">What works for post-acne marks in Indian skin?</h2>
      <p>Pigment responds to pigment treatments, and this is where Indian skin needs its own answer rather than a translated one. A systematic review of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in skin of colour notes that PIH is more prominent and more enduring in Fitzpatrick types III to VI, and an AI-based assessment of 20,914 Indian adults found types IV and V to be the most common in the population. A 2026 Delphi consensus published in the <em>Journal of Cosmetic Dermatology</em> recorded PIH among 27.3% of Indian participants surveyed against 15.1% globally — a clinician survey rather than a prevalence estimate, but pointing the same way.</p>
      <p>The practical evidence, including trials run in Indian skin:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Sunscreen first, every day.</strong> In the Indian Journal of Dermatology review of hyperpigmentation in the Indian population, sun exposure was the leading contributing factor at 59.2%. Without it the rest is wasted effort, and the marks that are fading will darken again.</li>
        <li><strong>Topical pigment agents.</strong> A randomised, double-blind, vehicle-controlled trial of 42 women in <em>Skin Research and Technology</em> (2014) found a combination of topical niacinamide and tranexamic acid reduced melanin index more than vehicle over eight weeks. A single-blinded randomised trial in 30 patients with acne-related PIH compared 20% azelaic acid cream with 5% tranexamic acid solution twice daily over 12 weeks and found both improved, with no severe side effects.</li>
        <li><strong>Superficial peels, chosen for pigment-prone skin.</strong> Garg and colleagues, in <em>Dermatologic Surgery</em> (2009), randomised 44 Indian patients with Fitzpatrick types IV to VI to 35% glycolic acid or 20% salicylic–10% mandelic acid peels. Both worked; the salicylic–mandelic peel did better on active acne and on hyperpigmentation, with fewer side effects. A separate randomised trial of 80 patients found 50% glycolic acid and 30% salicylic acid peels equally effective and safe for mild to moderate acne scarring in Indian skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Treat the acne that is still active.</strong> Every new inflamed lesion in pigment-prone skin is a new mark. Clearing the acne is the most reliable way to stop adding to the problem.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Note that these pigment trials are topical. There is no trial of an oral supplement using post-acne marks as an endpoint, which includes every oral ingredient we sell.</p>

      <h2 id="what-collagen-does">So what does a collagen shot actually do?</h2>
      <p>It acts on hydration, elasticity and overall tone, and those are the endpoints the trials have actually measured. A systematic review and meta-analysis of 10 randomised controlled trials in 646 participants reported better skin elasticity with collagen than placebo, at a standardised mean difference of 0.61 (95% CI 0.21 to 1.02). A second pooled analysis of four elasticity trials in 356 participants put the effect at 0.78, but with a confidence interval running from 0.12 to 1.45 and heterogeneity of 88.9% — a wide, unstable estimate. A 2025 systematic review of oral and topical peptides for skin ageing found no significant effect on elasticity at all. Taken together: a probable small benefit on elasticity and hydration, with trial quality and funding visibly influencing the answer. Our piece on <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">what collagen does and does not do to the body</a> covers that literature in more detail.</p>
      <p>None of those endpoints is a scar. Skin that holds moisture better and recovers a little more elasticity does not fill an ice pick pit, and it does not clear melanin.</p>

