      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When to see a dermatologist</h2>
      <p>Scarring is one of the few skin complaints where the cost of waiting is permanent, so this list is shorter and firmer than usual. Book an appointment if any of the following apply.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You have active inflamed or cystic acne alongside the marks. Treating the acne is what stops new scars forming, and it is the step a supplement cannot do.</li>
        <li>You can feel dips, pits or raised firm lumps. Texture change needs a procedure, chosen for your scar type and skin tone.</li>
        <li>Marks are getting darker or spreading rather than slowly fading over months.</li>
        <li>You are on, or have recently finished, oral isotretinoin. Peel and laser timing has to be planned around it; one published case documented severe hyperpigmentation and scarring after a glycolic acid peel combined with low-dose isotretinoin.</li>
        <li>You have tried treating it yourself for more than three months with nothing to show.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If hydration and dullness rather than scarring is what actually bothers you, our article on <a href="/pages/oral-hyaluronic-acid">oral hyaluronic acid</a> covers that evidence separately.</p>

