      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen fill in pitted acne scars?</summary>
          <p>No. A pitted or ice pick scar is a hole in the dermis held down by fibrous tissue, and no oral supplement has been shown to fill one. Fabbrocini and colleagues put ice pick scars at 60 to 70% of atrophic acne scars and note that they cannot disappear completely. Focal treatment in clinic is what addresses them.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do post-acne dark marks take to fade?</summary>
          <p>Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation usually fades over several months, and faster with daily sunscreen and a topical pigment agent. Indian Journal of Dermatology data show marks affect more than 70% of people under 35 with an acne history but fewer than 10% of those over 50, which reflects that most do eventually clear. Marks that are deepening rather than fading should be seen by a dermatologist.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen supplements cause acne?</summary>
          <p>There is no peer-reviewed trial showing that collagen peptides cause acne, and clinicians who have commented on it describe the reports as anecdotal. Where a supplement does seem to trigger breakouts, the usual suspects are added flavourings, sweeteners or a dairy or whey base rather than the collagen itself. Collabloom is marine collagen, stevia sweetened and contains no dairy.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is microneedling or laser better for acne scars?</summary>
          <p>The evidence does not clearly favour either as a single treatment. A 2025 meta-analysis comparing fractional CO2 laser with needling-based modalities found no statistically significant difference in mean scar reduction. What the larger 2026 network meta-analysis of 56 trials did show is that combinations, such as fractional CO2 laser with subcision, outperformed single treatments.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which peel is best for acne marks in Indian skin?</summary>
          <p>In a 2009 randomised study in Dermatologic Surgery of 44 Indian patients with Fitzpatrick types IV to VI, a 20% salicylic with 10% mandelic acid peel did better than 35% glycolic acid on both active acne and hyperpigmentation, with fewer side effects. A separate trial of 80 patients found 50% glycolic and 30% salicylic peels equally effective for mild to moderate acne scarring. Peel choice in pigment-prone skin should be made by a dermatologist.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take a collagen supplement at all if I have acne scars?</summary>
          <p>Only if you want what it has actually been measured for, which is hydration, elasticity and tone. Pooled trial data put the elasticity effect at a standardised mean difference of around 0.61, with wide confidence intervals and some reviews finding no effect. It will not treat your scars, and it is not a substitute for seeing someone about active acne.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do collagen injections work for acne scars, and are they the same thing?</summary>
          <p>They are not the same thing at all. Injectable fillers are placed into a scar in clinic, and the 2016 Cochrane review found moderate-quality evidence supporting them for acne scarring. Swallowing collagen does not deliver protein to a specific scar, so results from one say nothing about the other.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

